HHIISSTTOORRYY
       ffiinniitt__mmoodduullee()
              Linux 3.8.

       The  iinniitt__mmoodduullee()  system call is not supported by glibc.  No declara‐
       tion is provided in glibc headers, but, through  a  quirk  of  history,
       glibc  versions  before  glibc  2.23  did export an ABI for this system
       call.  Therefore, in order to employ this system call,  it  is  (before
       glibc  2.23) sufficient to manually declare the interface in your code;
       alternatively, you can invoke the system call using ssyyssccaallll(2).

   LLiinnuuxx 22..44 aanndd eeaarrlliieerr
       In Linux 2.4 and earlier, the iinniitt__mmoodduullee() system call was rather dif‐
       ferent:

           ##iinncclluuddee <<lliinnuuxx//mmoodduullee..hh>>

           iinntt iinniitt__mmoodduullee((ccoonnsstt cchhaarr **_n_a_m_e,, ssttrruucctt mmoodduullee **_i_m_a_g_e));;

       (User‐space applications can detect which version of  iinniitt__mmoodduullee()  is
       available by calling qquueerryy__mmoodduullee(); the latter call fails with the er‐
       ror EENNOOSSYYSS on Linux 2.6 and later.)

       The  older  version of the system call loads the relocated module image
       pointed to by _i_m_a_g_e into kernel space and runs the module’s _i_n_i_t  func‐
       tion.   The  caller  is  responsible  for providing the relocated image
       (since Linux 2.6, the iinniitt__mmoodduullee() system call does the relocation).

       The module image begins with a module structure and is followed by code
       and data as appropriate.  Since Linux 2.2, the module structure is  de‐
       fined as follows:

           struct module {
               unsigned long         size_of_struct;
               struct module        *next;
               const char           *name;
               unsigned long         size;
               long                  usecount;
               unsigned long         flags;
               unsigned int          nsyms;
               unsigned int          ndeps;
               struct module_symbol *syms;
               struct module_ref    *deps;
               struct module_ref    *refs;
               typeof(int (void))   *init;
               typeof(void (void))  *cleanup;
               const struct exception_table_entry *ex_table_start;
               const struct exception_table_entry *ex_table_end;
           #ifdef __alpha__
               unsigned long gp;
           #endif
           };

       All of the pointer fields, with the exception of _n_e_x_t and _r_e_f_s, are ex‐
       pected  to point within the module body and be initialized as appropri‐
       ate for kernel space, that is, relocated with the rest of the module.

